{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CbpGRFTCqWVYHebCpyXv4K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.85.2)\n2\t\n3\tEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\n4\tfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n5\t\n6\t**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\n7\tis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\n8\tare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\n9\tlacks.\n10\t\n11\t## Start here\n12\t\n13\t1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n14\t   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n15\t2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n16\t   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n17\t   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n18\t   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n19\t   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n20\t   `game/` and make it yours.\n21\t3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n22\t   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n23\t4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n24\t5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n25\t   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n26\t   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n27\t6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n28\t   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n29\t   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n30\t   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n31\t   before you use it.\n32\t\n33\t## What is here\n34\t\n35\t| File | What it is |\n36\t| --- | --- |\n37\t| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n38\t| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n39\t| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n40\t| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n41\t| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n42\t| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n43\t| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n44\t| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n45\t| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n46\t| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n47\t| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n48\t| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n49\t| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n50\t\n51\tNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n52\t(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\n53\tengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n54\t\n55\t## Run, test, check\n56\t\n57\t```sh\n58\tnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\n59\tnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\n60\tnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\n61\tnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\n62\tnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\n63\tnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\n64\tnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\tOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\n68\tthe two scripts into the page.\n69\t\n70\t## Testing a game without a browser\n71\t\n72\t`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped.\n73\t\n74\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n75\t- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n76\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n77\t  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n78\t- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n79\t\n80\tThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n81\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9eca6c66-8849-4edb-a442-cc89add0a71e","uuid":"882021c2-50c6-4e59-b982-7cb2079e1650","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:49:54.213Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/engine/dist/handoff/README.md","content":"# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.85.2)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page.\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CbpGRFTCqWVYHebCpyXv4K","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
