/README.md:571:  the browser, as `localStorage['ai-hub:relay-only']`).\n/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md:572:- While no game in the library declares `hub:session`, the reference games\n/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md:682:own, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md:689:an override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md:705:checks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md:706:(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"totalLines":12}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019qeQ3y2Gj14Uw83o452fKj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyKydC5zpfzksA2wAMn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VeywYNkHPVRwMUggxaZc9c","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md","offset":640,"limit":110},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1335,"cache_read_input_tokens":166551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1335},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9eca6c66-8849-4edb-a442-cc89add0a71e","uuid":"e1a2c814-7f7b-41f7-9474-fbe483f8a6b3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:51:45.541Z","request_id":"req_011CggyKyNZdTdjvLNVPvVT6","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VeywYNkHPVRwMUggxaZc9c":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md","offset":640,"limit":110}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VeywYNkHPVRwMUggxaZc9c","type":"tool_result","content":"640\t- Games run on the hub's own origin, so a game could read the signed-in\n641\t  user's Supabase token. Row-level security confines the damage to that\n642\t  user's own votes, plays and sessions, and Google's tokens are not kept.\n643\t  The real fix is serving games from a separate custom domain.\n644\t- Peer-to-peer play shows players' IP addresses to each other (`/privacy/`\n645\t  says so), unless a player turns on **Relay only** in the lobby.\n646\t\n647\t## Optional extras\n648\t\n649\tNone of this is needed. All of it is ignored if absent.\n650\t\n651\t**Hand-made cover art** — drop `covers/<slug>.png` (or `.jpg`, `.webp`) and it\n652\treplaces the generated art for that game. Slugs are listed in `data/games.json`.\n653\t\n654\t**Real screenshots** — `pnpm shots` opens each game, waits for its title screen\n655\tand saves a cover. Needs Playwright, which is not installed by default:\n656\t\n657\t```bash\n658\tpnpm add -D playwright && pnpm exec playwright install chromium\n659\tpnpm shots                # four at a time; --jobs 8 for more, --only <slug> for one\n660\t```\n661\t\n662\tA project with its own `og:image` already has a cover and is skipped.\n663\t\n664\t**Extra metadata** — `data/overrides.json`, keyed by slug, wins over anything\n665\tscraped:\n666\t\n667\t```json\n668\t{\n669\t  \"undertow\": {\n670\t    \"model\": \"Claude Opus 5\",\n671\t    \"harness\": \"Claude Code\",\n672\t    \"status\": \"polished\",\n673\t    \"tags\": [\"pixel-art\", \"dungeon\"]\n674\t  }\n675\t}\n676\t```\n677\t\n678\tHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\n679\tThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\n680\tgames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\n681\tAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\n682\town, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\n683\tvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\n684\tbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\n685\tthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\n686\thave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n687\t\n688\t`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\n689\tan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n690\t\n691\tThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n692\t\n693\t| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n694\t|---|---|---|\n695\t| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n696\t| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n697\t| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n698\t| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n699\t| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n700\t| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n701\t| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n702\t| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n703\t\n704\tEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\n705\tchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n706\t(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\n707\tGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\n708\trest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\n709\trecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\n710\twhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\n711\timport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\n712\tcurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\n713\timport that followed it.\n714\t\n715\tSetting `\"hidden\": true` drops a game from the catalogue entirely.\n716\t`\"restricted\": true` keeps it in the catalogue but off the wall, where only dev\n717\tsettings can bring it back — used for the companionship-business sims.\n718\t\n719\t### Dev settings\n720\t\n721\tThere is no button for them. **Shift + F2** opens them on a keyboard; on a\n722\tphone, tap the \"AI Hub\" title five times quickly. A PIN is asked first (any of\n723\t0000, 1111, 1234, 8888, 9999) and stays unlocked for that browser tab until\n724\tyou press Lock. Inside:\n725\t\n726\t- **Show all content** — restricted games appear, flagged *Hidden*, and the\n727\t  sign shows a reminder while it is on\n728\t- **Show slugs and file sizes on cabinets**\n729\t- **Open every game in a plain tab** — for a game that misbehaves in the frame\n730\t- **This device** — forget play history, favourites, or plain-tab choices\n731\t- **Library** — counts per shelf, how many are hidden, size, when the\n732\t  catalogue was built\n733\t\n734\tThis is a lock for a shared screen, not security: the PINs live in the page's\n735\tJavaScript, and every game is a public file.\n736\t\n737\t## How it is put together\n738\t\n739\t```\n740\tgames/            the drop zone\n741\tcovers/           optional art, by slug\n742\tsources/          historical source packages; new imports leave originals at input\n743\tdata/\n744\t  overrides.json  optional metadata, by slug\n745\t  added.json      first-seen dates, maintained automatically\n746\t  source-dates.json original source dates, used to order builds\n747\t  games.json      generated every build; gitignored, never edited\n748\tcouch/            the Couch: a page of its own, over the same library\n749\tcatalogue/        the Catalogue, likewise"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9eca6c66-8849-4edb-a442-cc89add0a71e","uuid":"8ae1f3d0-d44c-47b5-9565-a0da5749ff44","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:51:45.551Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/cc-plan-x4394d1/hub/README.md","content":"- Games run on the hub's own origin, so a game could read the signed-in\n  user's Supabase token. Row-level security confines the damage to that\n  user's own votes, plays and sessions, and Google's tokens are not kept.\n  The real fix is serving games from a separate custom domain.\n- Peer-to-peer play shows players' IP addresses to each other (`/privacy/`\n  says so), unless a player turns on **Relay only** in the lobby.\n\n## Optional extras\n\nNone of this is needed. All of it is ignored if absent.\n\n**Hand-made cover art** — drop `covers/<slug>.png` (or `.jpg`, `.webp`) and it\nreplaces the generated art for that game. Slugs are listed in `data/games.json`.\n\n**Real screenshots** — `pnpm shots` opens each game, waits for its title screen\nand saves a cover. Needs Playwright, which is not installed by default:\n\n```bash\npnpm add -D playwright && pnpm exec playwright install chromium\npnpm shots                # four at a time; --jobs 8 for more, --only <slug> for one\n```\n\nA project with its own `og:image` already has a cover and is skipped.\n\n**Extra metadata** — `data/overrides.json`, keyed by slug, wins over anything\nscraped:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"undertow\": {\n    \"model\": \"Claude Opus 5\",\n    \"harness\": \"Claude Code\",\n    \"status\": \"polished\",\n    \"tags\": [\"pixel-art\", \"dungeon\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\nown, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single player |\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\nimport that followed it.\n\nSetting `\"hidden\": true` drops a game from the catalogue entirely.\n`\"restricted\": true` keeps it in the catalogue but off the wall, where only dev\nsettings can bring it back — used for the companionship-business sims.\n\n### Dev settings\n\nThere is no button for them. **Shift + F2** opens them on a keyboard; on a\nphone, tap the \"AI Hub\" title five times quickly. A PIN is asked first (any of\n0000, 1111, 1234, 8888, 9999) and stays unlocked for that browser tab until\nyou press Lock. Inside:\n\n- **Show all content** — restricted games appear, flagged *Hidden*, and the\n  sign shows a reminder while it is on\n- **Show slugs and file sizes on cabinets**\n- **Open every game in a plain tab** — for a game that misbehaves in the frame\n- **This device** — forget play history, favourites, or plain-tab choices\n- **Library** — counts per shelf, how many are hidden, size, when the\n  catalogue was built\n\nThis is a lock for a shared screen, not security: the PINs live in the page's\nJavaScript, and every game is a public file.\n\n## How it is put together\n\n```\ngames/            the drop zone\ncovers/           optional art, by slug\nsources/          historical source packages; new imports leave originals at input\ndata/\n  overrides.json  optional metadata, by slug\n  added.json      first-seen dates, maintained automatically\n  source-dates.json original source dates, used to order builds\n  games.json      generated every build; gitignored, never edited\ncouch/            the Couch: a page of its own, over the same library\ncatalogue/        the Catalogue, likewise","numLines":110,"startLine":640,"totalLines":875}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VeywYNkHPVRwMUggxaZc9c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
