{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TvGub9oqbLRNvCEv5RnLCx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL\n2\t\n3\t**Over-the-top arcade football. The build plan for TASK.md.**\n4\t\n5\tThe title was picked the way TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"European football\" were brainstormed, then Python's\n6\t`secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy, not a seeded generator) chose index 0, **Europhoria Football** (Euro + euphoria).\n7\tUse exactly this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, the About screen, the AI Hub card, the exit interview.\n8\tDo not pick again.\n9\t\n10\t| | |\n11\t|---|---|\n12\t| **In one line** | A broke seaside club gets into the continent's biggest cup because of a typo, and its fans' euphoria carries it to the final. It plays as 5-a-side arcade football with super strikes, slide tackles that send people flying, dives, VAR, and a crowd that is your power bar. |\n13\t| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (4 outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run. 1 to 4 local players; online versus through AI Hub. |\n14\t| **Perspective** | A TV broadcast camera: side-on, raised 40° (a custom engine `View`), scrolling with play. Classic three-quarter (55°) and Aerial (80°) are options in Settings. Replays use turned angles. |\n15\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package (`dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), full engine, changed only as §5 lists. |\n16\t| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub `lib/hub`), mode `shared-world`, 2–4 players. |\n17\t| **Work root** | The folder that holds TASK.md, STAGE.md and this PLAN.md. |\n18\t| **Dev folder** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/` inside the work root. All dev files go here. |\n19\t| **Deliverable** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html` in the work root: one file, everything inlined, runs from `file://`. |\n20\t| **Exit interview** | `exit-interview.json` in the work root, next to the HTML, in TASK.md's exact shape. |\n21\t\n22\tWho this is for: the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Where this\n23\tplan and TASK.md seem to disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan gives a number, it is a starting value for tuning in\n24\tdev mode, not a law.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Contents\n29\t\n30\t1. [The game in one page](#1-the-game-in-one-page)\n31\t2. [Controls, prompts and devices](#2-controls-prompts-and-devices)\n32\t3. [Tech stack, folder layout, build](#3-tech-stack-folder-layout-build)\n33\t4. [Architecture](#4-architecture)\n34\t5. [Engine changes](#5-engine-changes)\n35\t6. [Systems and mechanics](#6-systems-and-mechanics)\n36\t7. [Content](#7-content)\n37\t8. [Art and animation](#8-art-and-animation)\n38\t9. [Build order and milestones](#9-build-order-and-milestones)\n39\t10. [Risks and how to de-risk them](#10-risks-and-how-to-de-risk-them)\n40\t11. [Testing](#11-testing)\n41\t12. [Definition of done and hand-back](#12-definition-of-done-and-hand-back)\n42\t- [Appendix A: tuning defaults (dev sliders)](#appendix-a-tuning-defaults-dev-sliders)\n43\t- [Appendix B: network messages](#appendix-b-network-messages)\n44\t- [Appendix C: data shapes](#appendix-c-data-shapes)\n45\t- [Appendix D: music sketches](#appendix-d-music-sketches)\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## 1. The game in one page\n50\t\n51\t### 1.1 Core loop\n52\t\n53\t> Win the ball, pass it about, beat your man, hit it like a train, and keep the crowd fed until they lift you into\n54\t> EUROPHORIA.\n55\t\n56\tEach match: possession, then chances, then the crowd's Euphoria rises, then a Europhoria burst and a Super Strike,\n57\tthen a goal, a celebration and a replay, then the kickoff again. Each cup round: a tabloid back page, a team talk, the\n58\tmatch, the result, then the next stop on the map.\n59\t\n60\t### 1.2 Pillars\n61\t\n62\t1. **Football first, circus second.** Passing, shooting, tackling and keeping must feel good with every over-the-top\n63\t   (OTT) extra switched off (dev mode has a \"pure football\" toggle). The OTT layer rewards good football; it never\n64\t   replaces it.\n65\t2. **The crowd is the power bar.** Euphoria is not an abstract meter: it is the noise of your fans. They chant, wave\n66\t   scarves, light flares and unfurl the tifo as it fills.\n67\t3. **Broadcast drama.** It is framed like football on TV: a side-on camera, a score bug, two commentators, replays,\n68\t   a fourth official's board, VAR.\n69\t4. **Couch and TV first.** A DualSense at 10 feet is the reference device. Big type, few choices per screen, glyph\n70\t   prompts, every screen usable with the d-pad and ✕/○. Keyboard and mouse are native too.\n71\t5. **Everything is data.** Teams, stadiums, story, commentary, drills, events, supers, chants and songs are entries in\n72\t   registries, so the rest of the game can be built out by adding data (§4.6).\n73\t\n74\t### 1.3 From the source to the mechanics\n75\t\n76\t| What makes European football itself | What it becomes in the game |\n77\t|---|---|\n78\t| Terraces, chants, scarves, flares, tifos | The Euphoria meter (§6.9), crowd states and chants (§6.18), the tifo and flares at Europhoria |\n79\t| Tactical schools: route one, tiki-taka, gegenpressing, catenaccio, galácticos | Each opponent's AI profile and gimmick (§6.13, §7.1); \"parking the bus\" is literal |\n80\t| Cold rainy nights, snow, the orange ball, floodlights | Weather that changes ball physics and visibility (§6.19) |\n81\t| Dives, cards, the referee, VAR | A Theatrics (dive) button, referee strictness, cards, advantage, VAR checks (§6.10) |\n82\t| Cup magic, giant-killing underdogs | The Cup Run story (§6.24, §7.3) |\n83\t| Tabloid back pages, commentary clichés, punditry | Tabloid screens between matches, two-voice commentary (§6.17, §7.4) |\n84\t| Sticker albums | The Sticker Album (§6.31, §7.5) |\n85\t| Goal celebrations | A celebration picker after every goal (§6.21) |\n86\t| Penalty heartbreak | Shootouts, the Panenka (§6.11, §6.12) |\n87\t| Nutmegs, step-overs, rainbow flicks, bicycle kicks, diving headers | Skill moves and the air-ball moves (§6.5, §6.6) |\n88\t| Action replays | Goal replays from turned camera angles (§6.15) |\n89\t| Pitch invaders, seagulls, beach balls on the pitch, floodlight failures | Matchday events (§6.20) |\n90\t\n91\t### 1.4 How long the demo is, and where each mechanic shows up first\n92\t\n93\tAt the default half length (2:00 of real time per half) a cup match takes about 6 minutes. The full demo is about 45–60\n94\tminutes: the cup run (5 matches and their story beats), 9 training drills, Kick-About, Penalties, the Sticker Album, and\n95\tthe attract mode. Every mechanic is required at some point in the cup run, in this order:\n96\t\n97\t| Mechanic | Taught in training drill | Required first in |\n98\t|---|---|---|\n99\t| Run, sprint, stamina, pass, shoot, power, aim | 1, 2, 3 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington) |\n100\t| Lobs, crosses, headers, volleys, corners, goal kicks | 4 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington are all about the air) |\n101\t| Tackles, slides, barges, switching, interceptions | 6 | Round of 16 (Pasapasa keep the ball) |\n102\t| Through balls, one-twos | 2 | Round of 16 |\n103\t| Skill moves, nutmegs; snow physics, the orange ball | 5 | Quarter-final (Stahlpresse press you) |\n104\t| Free kicks, penalties, cards, advantage, dives, VAR, the bus | 7, 9 | Semi-final (Lucchetto) |\n105\t| Euphoria, Europhoria, Super Strikes, the Save Duel | 8 | All matches; the final requires a Super Strike for its challenge |\n106\t| Golden goal, penalty shootout | 7 | Any drawn cup tie; Penalties mode; the attract mode shows one |\n107\t| Celebrations, replays, matchday events, weather | none | All matches |\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## 2. Controls, prompts and devices\n112\t\n113\t### 2.1 Action table\n114\t\n115\tThe reference pad is the PS5 DualSense in the browser's standard mapping (engine codes `Pad0`..`Pad16`). Prompts show\n116\tPlayStation glyphs by default; Settings → Controller → Button symbols switches to Xbox (A B X Y).\n117\t\n118\t| Action id | With the ball | Without the ball | Pad (PS / Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse (in play) |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| move | run / dribble | run | L-stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n121\t| `sprint` | sprint (stamina) | sprint | R2 / RT (`Pad7`) | Left Shift | — |\n122\t| `pass` | ground pass to the teammate the stick points at | standing tackle (poke) | ✕ / A (`Pad0`) | J | Left click: pass toward the cursor (click a teammate to pass to them) |\n123\t| `shoot` | hold to charge, release to shoot; stick aims | slide tackle | □ / X (`Pad2`) | K | Right button held: charge, release to shoot at the cursor |\n124\t| `lob` | lob, cross from wide, chip near the keeper | shoulder barge | ○ / B (`Pad1`) | L | Middle click: lob toward the cursor |\n125\t| `through` | through ball into space | switch player | △ / Y (`Pad3`) | I | — |\n126\t| `skill` | skill move (by stick direction, §6.6) | — | R1 / RB (`Pad5`) | E | — |\n127\t| `switch` | hold with pass: one-two | switch to the teammate nearest the ball | L1 / LB (`Pad4`) | Q | Left click on a teammate while defending: switch to them |\n128\t| `dive` | Theatrics (dive) | Theatrics | L2 / LT (`Pad6`) | C | — |\n129\t| `euphoria` | trigger Europhoria when the meter is full | same | L1 + R1 together | Space (also Q + E) | — |\n130\t| pause | pause menu (engine) | | Options (`Pad9`) | Esc, P | the corner's menu button |\n131\t| confirm / cancel | menus (engine) | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right click |\n132\t\n133\t- **Mouse aim (keyboard and mouse players).** When the mouse moves over the canvas in play, mouse aim switches on: a\n134\t  pixel reticle sits on the pitch under the pointer (`game.mouseGround()`). Passes, shots and lobs then aim at it, and\n135\t  moving still uses WASD. It switches off after 3 s without mouse movement, or when the stick is used. The wheel zooms\n136\t  the camera (the engine's own player zoom). In every menu the pointer's hover moves the focus and a click picks.\n137\t- **Celebrations and the Save Duel** reuse the face buttons (✕ □ ○ △ / J K L I).\n138\t- **Keepers** are AI-controlled except in penalties (§6.11) and the Save Duel (§6.9). After a catch, the human side's\n139\t  keeper waits up to 2 s for ✕ (throw toward the stick) or □ (kick long), then the AI distributes.\n140\t\n141\t### 2.2 Input preset\n142\t\n143\tDefine one preset, `EF.KEYS`, as a **fresh object**: first copy `up, down, left, right, start, pause, confirm, cancel`\n144\tfrom `E.Input.DEFAULT`, then add the game's actions **in this order**: `pass, shoot, lob, through, sprint, skill,\n145\tswitch, dive, euphoria` (the SPORTS kit's `Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {...})` would keep DEFAULT's own `skill`\n146\tkey in its old place and break the order). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives two players sharing one keyboard\n147\t8 buttons each in declaration order (left half F G H R T Y V B, right half . / , L ; ' K M, plus numpad 1–8). The\n148\tninth action, `euphoria`, gets no half-keyboard key, so on a shared keyboard Europhoria is the `switch` + `skill` chord\n149\t(the same chord as L1 + R1). Detect the chord in the controller: both down within 120 ms. A test checks the halves'\n150\tmapping (§11.3).\n151\t\n152\tMouse buttons: bind `Mouse0` to `pass`, `Mouse2` to `shoot`, `Mouse1` to `lob`, but read them only while mouse aim is\n153\ton, so a click on the canvas cannot fire a pass from a gamepad session.\n154\t\n155\t### 2.3 Prompts and glyphs\n156\t\n157\t- Prompts follow `game.input.lastDevice` (`'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`), per player\n158\t  (`game.players[i].lastDevice`).\n159\t- **Never pass pad symbols to `E.font`.** The pixel font is ASCII plus a few symbols and draws `'?'` for ✕ ○ □ △. Build\n160\t  `EF.Glyphs` (§4.2, `02-glyphs.js`): pixel-art icons drawn by code, 12×12 at scale 1 (24×24 at scale 2):\n161\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#6aa0ff`, ○ red `#ff5a5a`, □ pink `#ff7ad0`, △ green `#3ad6a0` on a dark disc; L1 R1 L2 R2 as\n162\t    rounded pills; Options and Create as small capsules; L and R sticks as discs with an arrow.\n163\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow discs with the letter; LB RB LT RT pills; Menu, View.\n164\t  - Keyboard: a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` (W, Shift, Space, J…), widened to fit.\n165\t  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left, right or middle button lit, and a wheel.\n166\t- `EF.prompt(r, x, y, action, label, { player, scale })` draws the icon for the action's binding on the player's\n167\t  device, then the label. `EF.promptWidth(...)` is there for centring. The binding comes from the input's map after the\n168\t  player's rebinding: take the first pad code or key code for the action (the logic of `input.label()`), then map it to\n169\t  an icon.\n170\t- **Default PlayStation.** In `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`, redefine the standard `glyphs` setting (a\n171\t  game's own settings are defined after the standard ones and replace a spec with the same key):\n172\t  `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Controller', label: 'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']], apply: (g, v) => { g.input.glyphs = v; for (const p of g.players) p.glyphs = v; } }`.\n173\t  Check that the Controls screen shows it under Controller. If it does not, change `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` in the\n174\t  engine instead (§5).\n175\t- Prompts on screen: context prompts near the controlled player (for example \"□ VOLLEY\" in the air-ball window, \"L1+R1\n176\t  EUROPHORIA!\" when the meter is full), set-piece prompts in a bottom bar, menu prompts in a footer\n177\t  (\"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK  L1/R1 PAGE\"). Settings → Game → Prompts: Always / While learning (the default: a prompt shows\n178\t  until the player has used that action 3 times) / Off.\n179\t\n180\t### 2.4 Shared keyboard, controllers joining\n181\t\n182\tKick-About's setup screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`: Start (Options) on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard\n183\t(player 2 takes the right half; player 1 moves to the left half by itself). Each player's icon then moves left (HOME),\n184\tcentre (CPU, which means not playing) or right (AWAY) with the stick or d-pad, and ✕ marks ready (§6.27). In the cup run,\n185\textra players can join the Kippers for co-op the same way on the pre-match team-talk screen.\n186\t\n187\t### 2.5 Touch-only devices\n188\t\n189\tThere are no touch controls. Every scene sets `touch: false` (no touch buttons), and `input.touchEnabled = false`. On a\n190\tdevice with a coarse pointer, no fine pointer and no gamepad, the title shows a panel: \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD\n191\tREQUIRED\". Under it is one big SETTINGS button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`), and the engine's corner menu button\n192\tstays. The attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap goes back to the title. A key press or a gamepad connecting\n193\tclears touch-only mode at once (§6.33).\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## 3. Tech stack, folder layout, build\n198\t\n199\t### 3.1 Stack\n200\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9eca6c66-8849-4edb-a442-cc89add0a71e","uuid":"30df0b17-312f-4013-9f31-cefc8c37d00f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:30:52.782Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174834__x4394d1/work/PLAN.md","content":"# EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL\n\n**Over-the-top arcade football. The build plan for TASK.md.**\n\nThe title was picked the way TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"European football\" were brainstormed, then Python's\n`secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy, not a seeded generator) chose index 0, **Europhoria Football** (Euro + euphoria).\nUse exactly this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, the About screen, the AI Hub card, the exit interview.\nDo not pick again.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **In one line** | A broke seaside club gets into the continent's biggest cup because of a typo, and its fans' euphoria carries it to the final. It plays as 5-a-side arcade football with super strikes, slide tackles that send people flying, dives, VAR, and a crowd that is your power bar. |\n| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (4 outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run. 1 to 4 local players; online versus through AI Hub. |\n| **Perspective** | A TV broadcast camera: side-on, raised 40° (a custom engine `View`), scrolling with play. Classic three-quarter (55°) and Aerial (80°) are options in Settings. Replays use turned angles. |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package (`dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), full engine, changed only as §5 lists. |\n| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub `lib/hub`), mode `shared-world`, 2–4 players. |\n| **Work root** | The folder that holds TASK.md, STAGE.md and this PLAN.md. |\n| **Dev folder** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/` inside the work root. All dev files go here. |\n| **Deliverable** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html` in the work root: one file, everything inlined, runs from `file://`. |\n| **Exit interview** | `exit-interview.json` in the work root, next to the HTML, in TASK.md's exact shape. |\n\nWho this is for: the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Where this\nplan and TASK.md seem to disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan gives a number, it is a starting value for tuning in\ndev mode, not a law.\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [The game in one page](#1-the-game-in-one-page)\n2. [Controls, prompts and devices](#2-controls-prompts-and-devices)\n3. [Tech stack, folder layout, build](#3-tech-stack-folder-layout-build)\n4. [Architecture](#4-architecture)\n5. [Engine changes](#5-engine-changes)\n6. [Systems and mechanics](#6-systems-and-mechanics)\n7. [Content](#7-content)\n8. [Art and animation](#8-art-and-animation)\n9. [Build order and milestones](#9-build-order-and-milestones)\n10. [Risks and how to de-risk them](#10-risks-and-how-to-de-risk-them)\n11. [Testing](#11-testing)\n12. [Definition of done and hand-back](#12-definition-of-done-and-hand-back)\n- [Appendix A: tuning defaults (dev sliders)](#appendix-a-tuning-defaults-dev-sliders)\n- [Appendix B: network messages](#appendix-b-network-messages)\n- [Appendix C: data shapes](#appendix-c-data-shapes)\n- [Appendix D: music sketches](#appendix-d-music-sketches)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n### 1.1 Core loop\n\n> Win the ball, pass it about, beat your man, hit it like a train, and keep the crowd fed until they lift you into\n> EUROPHORIA.\n\nEach match: possession, then chances, then the crowd's Euphoria rises, then a Europhoria burst and a Super Strike,\nthen a goal, a celebration and a replay, then the kickoff again. Each cup round: a tabloid back page, a team talk, the\nmatch, the result, then the next stop on the map.\n\n### 1.2 Pillars\n\n1. **Football first, circus second.** Passing, shooting, tackling and keeping must feel good with every over-the-top\n   (OTT) extra switched off (dev mode has a \"pure football\" toggle). The OTT layer rewards good football; it never\n   replaces it.\n2. **The crowd is the power bar.** Euphoria is not an abstract meter: it is the noise of your fans. They chant, wave\n   scarves, light flares and unfurl the tifo as it fills.\n3. **Broadcast drama.** It is framed like football on TV: a side-on camera, a score bug, two commentators, replays,\n   a fourth official's board, VAR.\n4. **Couch and TV first.** A DualSense at 10 feet is the reference device. Big type, few choices per screen, glyph\n   prompts, every screen usable with the d-pad and ✕/○. Keyboard and mouse are native too.\n5. **Everything is data.** Teams, stadiums, story, commentary, drills, events, supers, chants and songs are entries in\n   registries, so the rest of the game can be built out by adding data (§4.6).\n\n### 1.3 From the source to the mechanics\n\n| What makes European football itself | What it becomes in the game |\n|---|---|\n| Terraces, chants, scarves, flares, tifos | The Euphoria meter (§6.9), crowd states and chants (§6.18), the tifo and flares at Europhoria |\n| Tactical schools: route one, tiki-taka, gegenpressing, catenaccio, galácticos | Each opponent's AI profile and gimmick (§6.13, §7.1); \"parking the bus\" is literal |\n| Cold rainy nights, snow, the orange ball, floodlights | Weather that changes ball physics and visibility (§6.19) |\n| Dives, cards, the referee, VAR | A Theatrics (dive) button, referee strictness, cards, advantage, VAR checks (§6.10) |\n| Cup magic, giant-killing underdogs | The Cup Run story (§6.24, §7.3) |\n| Tabloid back pages, commentary clichés, punditry | Tabloid screens between matches, two-voice commentary (§6.17, §7.4) |\n| Sticker albums | The Sticker Album (§6.31, §7.5) |\n| Goal celebrations | A celebration picker after every goal (§6.21) |\n| Penalty heartbreak | Shootouts, the Panenka (§6.11, §6.12) |\n| Nutmegs, step-overs, rainbow flicks, bicycle kicks, diving headers | Skill moves and the air-ball moves (§6.5, §6.6) |\n| Action replays | Goal replays from turned camera angles (§6.15) |\n| Pitch invaders, seagulls, beach balls on the pitch, floodlight failures | Matchday events (§6.20) |\n\n### 1.4 How long the demo is, and where each mechanic shows up first\n\nAt the default half length (2:00 of real time per half) a cup match takes about 6 minutes. The full demo is about 45–60\nminutes: the cup run (5 matches and their story beats), 9 training drills, Kick-About, Penalties, the Sticker Album, and\nthe attract mode. Every mechanic is required at some point in the cup run, in this order:\n\n| Mechanic | Taught in training drill | Required first in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run, sprint, stamina, pass, shoot, power, aim | 1, 2, 3 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington) |\n| Lobs, crosses, headers, volleys, corners, goal kicks | 4 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington are all about the air) |\n| Tackles, slides, barges, switching, interceptions | 6 | Round of 16 (Pasapasa keep the ball) |\n| Through balls, one-twos | 2 | Round of 16 |\n| Skill moves, nutmegs; snow physics, the orange ball | 5 | Quarter-final (Stahlpresse press you) |\n| Free kicks, penalties, cards, advantage, dives, VAR, the bus | 7, 9 | Semi-final (Lucchetto) |\n| Euphoria, Europhoria, Super Strikes, the Save Duel | 8 | All matches; the final requires a Super Strike for its challenge |\n| Golden goal, penalty shootout | 7 | Any drawn cup tie; Penalties mode; the attract mode shows one |\n| Celebrations, replays, matchday events, weather | none | All matches |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls, prompts and devices\n\n### 2.1 Action table\n\nThe reference pad is the PS5 DualSense in the browser's standard mapping (engine codes `Pad0`..`Pad16`). Prompts show\nPlayStation glyphs by default; Settings → Controller → Button symbols switches to Xbox (A B X Y).\n\n| Action id | With the ball | Without the ball | Pad (PS / Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse (in play) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move | run / dribble | run | L-stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n| `sprint` | sprint (stamina) | sprint | R2 / RT (`Pad7`) | Left Shift | — |\n| `pass` | ground pass to the teammate the stick points at | standing tackle (poke) | ✕ / A (`Pad0`) | J | Left click: pass toward the cursor (click a teammate to pass to them) |\n| `shoot` | hold to charge, release to shoot; stick aims | slide tackle | □ / X (`Pad2`) | K | Right button held: charge, release to shoot at the cursor |\n| `lob` | lob, cross from wide, chip near the keeper | shoulder barge | ○ / B (`Pad1`) | L | Middle click: lob toward the cursor |\n| `through` | through ball into space | switch player | △ / Y (`Pad3`) | I | — |\n| `skill` | skill move (by stick direction, §6.6) | — | R1 / RB (`Pad5`) | E | — |\n| `switch` | hold with pass: one-two | switch to the teammate nearest the ball | L1 / LB (`Pad4`) | Q | Left click on a teammate while defending: switch to them |\n| `dive` | Theatrics (dive) | Theatrics | L2 / LT (`Pad6`) | C | — |\n| `euphoria` | trigger Europhoria when the meter is full | same | L1 + R1 together | Space (also Q + E) | — |\n| pause | pause menu (engine) | | Options (`Pad9`) | Esc, P | the corner's menu button |\n| confirm / cancel | menus (engine) | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right click |\n\n- **Mouse aim (keyboard and mouse players).** When the mouse moves over the canvas in play, mouse aim switches on: a\n  pixel reticle sits on the pitch under the pointer (`game.mouseGround()`). Passes, shots and lobs then aim at it, and\n  moving still uses WASD. It switches off after 3 s without mouse movement, or when the stick is used. The wheel zooms\n  the camera (the engine's own player zoom). In every menu the pointer's hover moves the focus and a click picks.\n- **Celebrations and the Save Duel** reuse the face buttons (✕ □ ○ △ / J K L I).\n- **Keepers** are AI-controlled except in penalties (§6.11) and the Save Duel (§6.9). After a catch, the human side's\n  keeper waits up to 2 s for ✕ (throw toward the stick) or □ (kick long), then the AI distributes.\n\n### 2.2 Input preset\n\nDefine one preset, `EF.KEYS`, as a **fresh object**: first copy `up, down, left, right, start, pause, confirm, cancel`\nfrom `E.Input.DEFAULT`, then add the game's actions **in this order**: `pass, shoot, lob, through, sprint, skill,\nswitch, dive, euphoria` (the SPORTS kit's `Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {...})` would keep DEFAULT's own `skill`\nkey in its old place and break the order). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives two players sharing one keyboard\n8 buttons each in declaration order (left half F G H R T Y V B, right half . / , L ; ' K M, plus numpad 1–8). The\nninth action, `euphoria`, gets no half-keyboard key, so on a shared keyboard Europhoria is the `switch` + `skill` chord\n(the same chord as L1 + R1). Detect the chord in the controller: both down within 120 ms. A test checks the halves'\nmapping (§11.3).\n\nMouse buttons: bind `Mouse0` to `pass`, `Mouse2` to `shoot`, `Mouse1` to `lob`, but read them only while mouse aim is\non, so a click on the canvas cannot fire a pass from a gamepad session.\n\n### 2.3 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Prompts follow `game.input.lastDevice` (`'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`), per player\n  (`game.players[i].lastDevice`).\n- **Never pass pad symbols to `E.font`.** The pixel font is ASCII plus a few symbols and draws `'?'` for ✕ ○ □ △. Build\n  `EF.Glyphs` (§4.2, `02-glyphs.js`): pixel-art icons drawn by code, 12×12 at scale 1 (24×24 at scale 2):\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#6aa0ff`, ○ red `#ff5a5a`, □ pink `#ff7ad0`, △ green `#3ad6a0` on a dark disc; L1 R1 L2 R2 as\n    rounded pills; Options and Create as small capsules; L and R sticks as discs with an arrow.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow discs with the letter; LB RB LT RT pills; Menu, View.\n  - Keyboard: a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` (W, Shift, Space, J…), widened to fit.\n  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left, right or middle button lit, and a wheel.\n- `EF.prompt(r, x, y, action, label, { player, scale })` draws the icon for the action's binding on the player's\n  device, then the label. `EF.promptWidth(...)` is there for centring. The binding comes from the input's map after the\n  player's rebinding: take the first pad code or key code for the action (the logic of `input.label()`), then map it to\n  an icon.\n- **Default PlayStation.** In `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`, redefine the standard `glyphs` setting (a\n  game's own settings are defined after the standard ones and replace a spec with the same key):\n  `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Controller', label: 'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']], apply: (g, v) => { g.input.glyphs = v; for (const p of g.players) p.glyphs = v; } }`.\n  Check that the Controls screen shows it under Controller. If it does not, change `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` in the\n  engine instead (§5).\n- Prompts on screen: context prompts near the controlled player (for example \"□ VOLLEY\" in the air-ball window, \"L1+R1\n  EUROPHORIA!\" when the meter is full), set-piece prompts in a bottom bar, menu prompts in a footer\n  (\"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK  L1/R1 PAGE\"). Settings → Game → Prompts: Always / While learning (the default: a prompt shows\n  until the player has used that action 3 times) / Off.\n\n### 2.4 Shared keyboard, controllers joining\n\nKick-About's setup screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`: Start (Options) on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard\n(player 2 takes the right half; player 1 moves to the left half by itself). Each player's icon then moves left (HOME),\ncentre (CPU, which means not playing) or right (AWAY) with the stick or d-pad, and ✕ marks ready (§6.27). In the cup run,\nextra players can join the Kippers for co-op the same way on the pre-match team-talk screen.\n\n### 2.5 Touch-only devices\n\nThere are no touch controls. Every scene sets `touch: false` (no touch buttons), and `input.touchEnabled = false`. On a\ndevice with a coarse pointer, no fine pointer and no gamepad, the title shows a panel: \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD\nREQUIRED\". Under it is one big SETTINGS button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`), and the engine's corner menu button\nstays. The attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap goes back to the title. A key press or a gamepad connecting\nclears touch-only mode at once (§6.33).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack, folder layout, build\n\n### 3.1 Stack\n","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2140}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TvGub9oqbLRNvCEv5RnLCx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
